                <fr:title text="Syntax Category">Syntax Category</fr:title>
                <fr:taxon>Definition</fr:taxon>
                <fr:meta name="source">
                  <fr:link href="/dougherty-1993.xml" title="Closed Categories and Categorial Grammar" addr="dougherty-1993" type="local">[dougherty-1993]</fr:link>
                </fr:meta>
              </fr:frontmatter>
              <fr:mainmatter>
                <html:p>Fixing a set of base types <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[B]]></fr:tex>,

<html:ul><html:li><fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[Typ]]></fr:tex> is the set of type expressions generated from <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[B]]></fr:tex> over <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[I, {} \multimap _L {}, {} \multimap _R {}]]></fr:tex></html:li>
  <html:li>A <html:em>lexicon</html:em> is a set of entries of the from <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[[ \alpha  : a ]]]></fr:tex>, where <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\alpha ]]></fr:tex> is a <html:em>lexical item</html:em> and <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[a \in  Typ]]></fr:tex></html:li>
  <html:li>A <html:em>syntax tree</html:em> is a binary tree whose leaves are lexical items</html:li>
  <html:li>As <html:em>phrase</html:em> is a syntax tree with a linear ordering on its leaves</html:li></html:ul></html:p>
                <html:p>A <html:em>syntax category</html:em> over base types <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[B]]></fr:tex> and lexicon <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[Lex]]></fr:tex> is a <fr:link href="/sss-0005.xml" title="Biclosed Monoidal Category" addr="sss-0005" type="local">closed category</fr:link> <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[C]]></fr:tex> that assigns an object <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\omega  (b)]]></fr:tex> to each <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[b \in  B]]></fr:tex>, and a mapping <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\mu ]]></fr:tex> from <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[Lex]]></fr:tex> to arrows of <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[C]]></fr:tex>. That is, <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\mu  (\alpha  : a) : C[ 1 , \omega  (\alpha ) ]]]></fr:tex>.</html:p>
              </fr:mainmatter>
            </fr:tree>
            <html:p>This work makes it more precise that closed monoidal categories form a natural setting for syntactic reasoning. However, it is perhaps still a little underwhelming and there is room for improvement. The correspondence to our ongoing work is:

<html:ul><html:li>Intuitively we can read <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[Typ]]></fr:tex> as giving the objects of a typing category</html:li>
<html:li>A lexicon behaves like a context with assignments to each variable. We may think of these assignments of variables to lexical items as giving sentence to be parsed</html:li>
<html:li>A syntax tree is a proof that the phrase given by the lexicon belongs to the grammar.</html:li></html:ul></html:p>
